{
  "dom_id": "step-486644",
  "name": "Expressing Sums in Sigma Notation",
  "problem": "Suppose we want to express the following series in sigma notation.\n\n$$\nS = \\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4} + \\cdots + \\dfrac{\\color{red}19}{\\color{blue}20} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}20}{\\color{blue}21}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we first examine the numerators and denominators of the corresponding sequence.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}, \\quad \\ldots, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}19}{\\color{blue}20}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}20}{\\color{blue}21}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find a pattern in the terms. To do this, we note the following:\n\n- The numerators form the following sequence:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}1}, \\quad {\\color{red}2}, \\quad {\\color{red}3}, \\quad \\dots, \\quad {\\color{red}19}, \\quad {\\color{red}20}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $20$ terms in total. The $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}a_n} = {\\color{red}n}, \\qquad 1\\leq n \\leq 20.\n$$\n\n- The denominators form the following sequence:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad {\\color{blue}3}, \\quad \\dots, \\quad {\\color{blue}20}, \\quad {\\color{blue}21}\n$$\n\nSince the value in the denominator is always *one more than* the numerator, the $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}b_n} = {\\color{blue}n + 1} , \\qquad 1\\leq n \\leq 20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}, \\quad \\ldots, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}19}{\\color{blue}20}, \\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}20}{\\color{blue}21}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}a_n}{\\color{blue}b_n} =\\dfrac{\\color{red}n}{\\color{blue}n+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to $n=20.$ \n\nTherefore, we can express our series using sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{20}\\frac{n}{n+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by writing out the summation in full and checking that we recover the original sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{20}\\frac{n}{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+1}+\\frac{2}{2+1}+⋯+\\frac{19}{19+1}+\\frac{20}{20+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}+⋯+\\frac{19}{20}+\\frac{20}{21}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
